// MP with a fenced writer and an address-dependent reader.
//
// rmm-zoo property: mca (Atomicity) -- positive control for dependency ordering.
// Source: rmm-zoo litmus/strictly-weaker/POWER-vs-Coherence/MP+sync+addr and
//         litmus/strictly-weaker/ARM-vs-Coherence/MP+dmb+addr;
//         Alglave et al., "Herding Cats" (TOPLAS 2014).
//
// This is the shape the zoo uses to separate real hardware models from bare
// Coherence: the writer is fenced and the reader's two loads are ordered by an
// address dependency, so both POWER and ARM forbid the stale read, while a model
// that ignores dependencies allows it. Included here because it is the control
// that makes the MCA verdicts in this directory meaningful -- a model that
// allowed THIS is not preserving dependencies at all, and the IRIW+addrs /
// WRC+data+addr results would say nothing about multicopy atomicity.
//
// Reference verdicts: POWER, ARM, ARMv8, SC FORBID. Bare Coherence ALLOWS.
x := 0;
y := 0;
{
  x := 1;
  fence(sc);
  y := 1
} ||| {
  r1 := y;
  rq := &x;
  r2 := *(rq + (r1 - r1))
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 0) [Power]
